Pillage of directories works better on Windows: unreadable folders and locked files are skipped with a warning instead of stopping the run, directory junctions and links are no longer followed (OneDrive folders and other reparse points that aren't links are still searched), and the excluded top-level folders (e.g. ``Windows``) are matched case-insensitively and are also skipped when the source is an absolute path. ``peat pillage`` without ``-P`` now searches the current directory as documented instead of failing, and PEAT's own output (``pillage_results`` and ``peat_results``) is no longer searched.
